# REGRESSION: `Box[T](v)` must mint a box whose element type is T, for EVERY
# primitive T — one typedef, one alloc helper and one free fn per element type.
#
# The ctor mint used to derive its element C name from a lookup that knows only
# NAMED types and defaulted to `int64_t` for everything else, so every unnamed
# element collapsed onto a single `Box__int64_t` whose body was generated by
# whichever primitive reached the mint first. The C lane then silently printed
# the wrong value; the LLVM lane hard-errored in `llc`
# (`'%v3' defined with type 'i64' but expected 'double'`) because its typed IR
# will not let two different element types share one alloc signature.
#
# ⛔ THE DECLARATION ORDER BELOW IS LOAD-BEARING — DO NOT SORT OR REORDER IT.
# `float` MUST COME FIRST. The wrong-answer default spelt `int64_t`, so a cell
# whose element genuinely IS 64-bit-integer-shaped is correct BY ACCIDENT under
# the defect. With `Box[int]` leading, every later box inherits an `int64_t`
# typedef that happens to be right for the leader and this whole fixture is
# GREEN ON ARRIVAL — it would pin nothing (Core #12: a fixture must be seen to
# FAIL). Leading with a `double` element makes the collapse observable on the
# very next line.
#
# ⛔ THE AXIS HAS ELEVEN CELLS AND THIS FIXTURE COVERS TEN. The omitted one is
# `float32`, and it is NAMED here rather than left to be discovered, because a
# net that quietly samples ten of eleven is the same anecdote it was written to
# replace (Core #12: cover every value, or name each omitted cell).
#
# WHY `float32` IS OUT, and it is not "one more row": it fails on a DIFFERENT
# axis and would make this fixture assert two unrelated things at once. Here
# the defect was the element NAME — every unnamed element collapsing onto one
# `Box__int64_t`. For `float32` the name is derived CORRECTLY as `"float"`, and
# the generated `__gorget_box_alloc_float` BODY takes and stores a `double`, so
# the value is written eight bytes wide and read back four: C prints
# `0.000000`, LLVM refuses at `llc`. Measured identical before and after the
# element-naming fix — the `float32` arm was never reached by it.
# Pinned separately, with its own repro and its own `#[ignore]`d test:
# `tests/fixtures/known_gaps/box_float32_element_reads_zero.gg`.

void main():
    Box[float] a = Box[float](1.5)
    Box[uint8] b = Box[uint8](200)
    Box[int8] c = Box[int8](7)
    Box[int16] d = Box[int16](300)
    Box[uint16] e = Box[uint16](60000)
    Box[int32] f = Box[int32](70000)
    Box[uint32] g = Box[uint32](4000000)
    Box[int] h = Box[int](5000000000)
    Box[bool] i = Box[bool](true)
    Box[uint64] j = Box[uint64](42)
    print((*a))
    print((*b))
    print((*c))
    print((*d))
    print((*e))
    print((*f))
    print((*g))
    print((*h))
    print((*i))
    print((*j))
